INSERT INTO questions (category, type, question, options, answer, explanation, sheet) VALUES
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the result of bool(0) in Python?$q$, $j$["True", "False", "None", "Error"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$In Python, bool(0) evaluates to False because 0 is falsy. Any non-zero number evaluates to True. This is part of Python's truthiness rules.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which of the following correctly swaps two variables a and b in Python?$q$, $j$["a = b; b = a", "temp = a; a = b; b = temp", "a, b = b, a", "Both B and C are correct"]$j$::jsonb, 3, $q$Python supports tuple unpacking for swapping: a, b = b, a is the idiomatic way. The classic temp-variable approach (B) also works. Option A fails because a loses its original value before b is updated.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the // operator do in Python?$q$, $j$["Regular division", "Floor (integer) division", "Modulo", "Exponentiation"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The // operator performs floor division, which divides and rounds down to the nearest integer. For example, 7 // 2 gives 3, not 3.5.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the output of type(3.14) in Python?$q$, $j$["<class 'int'>", "<class 'double'>", "<class 'float'>", "<class 'decimal'>"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$In Python, decimal numbers are of type float. There is no 'double' or 'decimal' built-in type. type(3.14) returns <class 'float'>.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which of the following is a mutable data type in Python?$q$, $j$["tuple", "string", "int", "list"]$j$::jsonb, 3, $q$Lists are mutable — their contents can be changed after creation. Strings, tuples, and integers are immutable; once created their values cannot be modified in-place.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What will the following code print?

for i in range(3):
    print(i, end=' ')$q$, $j$["1 2 3", "0 1 2", "0 1 2 3", "1 2"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$range(3) generates 0, 1, 2. The end=' ' argument replaces the default newline with a space, so all values print on one line: 0 1 2.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the correct way to check if a key exists in a dictionary?$q$, $j$["dict.haskey('key')", "'key' in dict", "dict.contains('key')", "dict.exists('key')"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The 'in' operator is used to check membership in a dictionary (checks keys). dict.has_key() was removed in Python 3, and haskey/contains/exists are not valid dictionary methods.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the pop() method do when called on a list without arguments?$q$, $j$["Removes the first element", "Removes the last element and returns it", "Removes all elements", "Raises an error"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$list.pop() without arguments removes and returns the last element. You can also pass an index, e.g. list.pop(0) removes the first element.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which of the following creates an empty set in Python?$q$, $j$["{}", "set()", "[]", "()"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q${} creates an empty dictionary, not a set. To create an empty set you must use set(). This is a common Python gotcha.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the output of 'Python'[::-1]?$q$, $j$["Python", "nohtyP", "Pytho", "Error"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The slice [::-1] reverses a string by stepping through it backwards. 'Python'[::-1] produces 'nohtyP'.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the 'pass' statement do in Python?$q$, $j$["Exits the current loop", "Skips the current iteration", "Acts as a placeholder that does nothing", "Returns None from a function"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$The 'pass' statement is a no-operation placeholder. It is used when a block is syntactically required but you don't want any code to execute, for example in empty class or function definitions.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between '==' and 'is' in Python?$q$, $j$["They are identical", "'==' checks value equality; 'is' checks identity (same object in memory)", "'is' checks value; '==' checks identity", "'is' only works for numbers"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$'==' compares values: 1 == 1.0 is True. 'is' checks if two variables point to the exact same object in memory. For example, [1,2] == [1,2] is True but [1,2] is [1,2] is False.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the output of len([1, [2, 3], 4])?$q$, $j$["4", "3", "5", "2"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$len() counts the top-level elements of a list. The list has 3 top-level elements: 1, [2,3], and 4. The nested list [2,3] counts as one element.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which of the following will produce a TypeError in Python?$q$, $j$["'5' + '3'", "5 + 3", "'5' * 3", "'5' + 3"]$j$::jsonb, 3, $q$You cannot concatenate a string and an integer directly. '5' + 3 raises a TypeError. '5' + '3' gives '53', 5 + 3 gives 8, and '5' * 3 gives '555'.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the 'continue' statement do inside a loop?$q$, $j$["Exits the loop immediately", "Skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next", "Pauses execution", "Repeats the current iteration"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The 'continue' statement skips any remaining code in the current loop iteration and jumps to the next iteration. Use 'break' to exit the loop entirely.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is a default argument in a Python function?$q$, $j$["An argument that must always be provided", "An argument with a pre-set value used when no value is passed", "The first argument of any function", "An argument passed by reference"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$A default argument has a fallback value defined in the function signature, e.g. def greet(name='World'). If no value is passed for that argument, the default is used.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the output of print(2 ** 3 ** 2)?$q$, $j$["64", "512", "36", "8"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Exponentiation (**) is right-associative in Python, so 2 ** 3 ** 2 is evaluated as 2 ** (3 ** 2) = 2 ** 9 = 512.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which method can be used to find the index of an element in a list?$q$, $j$["list.find()", "list.search()", "list.index()", "list.locate()"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$list.index(value) returns the index of the first occurrence of the value. If not found it raises a ValueError. find() exists for strings but not lists.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the 'global' keyword do inside a function?$q$, $j$["Creates a new global variable", "Allows the function to read and modify a variable defined at the module level", "Makes a variable accessible only inside the function", "Imports all global variables"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Without 'global', assigning to a variable inside a function creates a local variable. The 'global' keyword tells Python to use the module-level variable instead of creating a local one.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the result of 'hello'.capitalize()?$q$, $j$["HELLO", "Hello", "hELLO", "hello"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$capitalize() converts the first character to uppercase and the rest to lowercase. 'hello'.capitalize() returns 'Hello'. It also lowercases any already-uppercase letters after the first.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the __str__ method do in a Python class?$q$, $j$["Returns the memory address of the object", "Returns a user-friendly string representation of the object", "Converts all attributes to strings", "It is called automatically when the object is created"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$__str__ defines the string representation shown when you call print() or str() on an object. It is meant to be human-readable, unlike __repr__ which is for debugging.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the output of the following code?

class Animal:
    sound = 'generic'

dog = Animal()
dog.sound = 'woof'
print(Animal.sound)$q$, $j$["woof", "generic", "None", "Error"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Assigning dog.sound = 'woof' creates an instance attribute on dog, it does NOT change the class attribute. Animal.sound remains 'generic'.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is method overriding in Python?$q$, $j$["Defining two methods with the same name but different parameters in the same class", "Redefining a parent class method in a subclass with new behavior", "Calling a method multiple times", "Making a method private"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation of a method already defined in its parent class. Python uses the most specific (subclass) version when the method is called on a subclass instance.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which exception is raised when you try to open a file that does not exist in read mode?$q$, $j$["ValueError", "FileError", "FileNotFoundError", "IOError"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$In Python 3, trying to open a non-existent file with open('file.txt', 'r') raises FileNotFoundError, which is a subclass of OSError. IOError is an older alias.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of the 'finally' block in exception handling?$q$, $j$["It runs only if no exception occurred", "It runs only if an exception occurred", "It always runs regardless of whether an exception occurred or not", "It re-raises the exception"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$The 'finally' block always executes, whether or not an exception was raised. It is typically used for cleanup operations like closing files or releasing resources.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the 'with' statement guarantee when used with file operations?$q$, $j$["The file is read line by line", "The file is automatically closed even if an exception occurs", "The file is opened in binary mode", "The file cannot be modified"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The 'with' statement uses a context manager. For files, it guarantees the file is closed properly when the block exits, even if an exception is raised inside the block.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is multiple inheritance in Python?$q$, $j$["A class that defines multiple methods", "A class that inherits from more than one parent class", "Inheriting the same class multiple times", "A method that overrides multiple parent methods"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiple inheritance allows a class to inherit attributes and methods from more than one parent class. For example: class C(A, B). Python uses the MRO (Method Resolution Order) to resolve conflicts.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is encapsulation in object-oriented programming?$q$, $j$["Inheriting properties from a parent class", "Bundling data and methods together and restricting direct access to some components", "Creating multiple objects from one class", "Defining multiple methods with the same name"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Encapsulation is the OOP principle of bundling data (attributes) and methods that operate on that data within a class, and restricting external direct access. In Python, this is done with single (_) or double (__) underscores.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does csv.writer() do?$q$, $j$["Reads a CSV file into a list", "Creates a writer object to write rows to a CSV file", "Converts a list to a CSV string", "Opens a CSV file for editing"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$csv.writer() creates a writer object that converts data to CSV format. You then call writer.writerow() or writer.writerows() to write data to the file.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is a list comprehension in Python?$q$, $j$["A way to understand what a list contains", "A concise syntax for creating a new list by applying an expression to each item in an iterable", "A method to sort a list", "A way to merge two lists"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$List comprehensions provide a compact way to create lists: [expr for item in iterable if condition]. For example, [x**2 for x in range(5)] creates [0, 1, 4, 9, 16].$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between instance methods and static methods in Python?$q$, $j$["There is no difference", "Instance methods take 'self' as the first argument; static methods do not and cannot access instance or class data", "Static methods are faster", "Instance methods cannot access class attributes"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Instance methods take 'self' and can access instance and class data. Static methods (decorated with @staticmethod) take no self/cls argument and are essentially regular functions namespaced inside a class.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the seek(0) method do on a file object?$q$, $j$["Closes the file", "Moves the file pointer to the beginning of the file", "Reads the first byte", "Checks if the file is empty"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$seek(offset) moves the file's read/write pointer to a given byte position. seek(0) resets it to the beginning so you can read the file again from the start.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which decorator is used to define a class method in Python?$q$, $j$["@staticmethod", "@classmethod", "@property", "@instancemethod"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$@classmethod makes a method receive the class (cls) as its first argument instead of an instance. It can access and modify class state. @staticmethod methods receive neither self nor cls.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What happens when you try to access an attribute that doesn't exist on an object in Python?$q$, $j$["It returns None", "It returns an empty string", "It raises an AttributeError", "It creates the attribute automatically"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$Accessing a non-existent attribute raises AttributeError. You can avoid this with hasattr(obj, 'attr') or getattr(obj, 'attr', default) to provide a fallback value.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of __del__ in a Python class?$q$, $j$["Deletes a specific attribute", "Is called when the object is about to be garbage collected (destructor)", "Deletes the class from memory", "Removes the last element of a list attribute"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$__del__ is the destructor method. It is called when an object is about to be destroyed by the garbage collector. It is rarely used in Python since memory is managed automatically.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the 'r+' file mode do?$q$, $j$["Opens for reading only", "Opens for writing only and creates the file if it doesn't exist", "Opens for both reading and writing; the file must already exist", "Opens for reading and appending"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$'r+' opens an existing file for both reading and writing. Unlike 'w+', it does NOT truncate the file or create it if it doesn't exist — the file must already exist.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is polymorphism in OOP?$q$, $j$["A class having many attributes", "The ability of different objects to respond to the same method call in different ways", "Inheriting from multiple classes", "Hiding internal data from outside code"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Polymorphism allows objects of different classes to be treated as objects of a common interface. For example, both Dog and Cat classes may have a speak() method that behaves differently for each.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does writelines() do when writing to a file?$q$, $j$["Writes a single line with a newline at the end", "Writes a list of strings to the file without adding newlines automatically", "Writes each element on a new line automatically", "Appends lines to the end of the file"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$writelines() accepts an iterable of strings and writes them to the file. It does NOT add newlines automatically — you must include '\n' in the strings yourself if needed.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the use of the 'super()' function in Python?$q$, $j$["Creates a superclass", "Returns a proxy object that allows calling parent class methods", "Checks if a class is a superclass", "Makes all parent class methods available as static methods"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$super() returns a proxy object that delegates method calls to a parent class. It is commonly used in __init__ to call the parent constructor: super().__init__() — ensuring the parent is properly initialized.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does pickle.load() do?$q$, $j$["Serializes a Python object to a file", "Deserializes (reads and reconstructs) a Python object from a binary file", "Loads a text file into memory", "Converts a Python object to JSON"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$pickle.load(file) reads a pickled binary file and reconstructs the original Python object. pickle.dump() is the opposite — it serializes an object to a file.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the bias-variance tradeoff in machine learning?$q$, $j$["The tradeoff between model size and training speed", "The balance between a model's ability to fit training data (low bias) and generalize to new data (low variance)", "The balance between accuracy and precision metrics", "The tradeoff between supervised and unsupervised learning"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$High bias means the model is too simple (underfitting). High variance means the model is too complex (overfitting). The bias-variance tradeoff is finding the right complexity that minimizes total error on unseen data.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of a validation set in machine learning?$q$, $j$["It is used to train the final model", "It is used to tune hyperparameters and evaluate the model during development without touching the test set", "It replaces the test set", "It is used only in unsupervised learning"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$A validation set is held out during training to tune hyperparameters and compare different models. The test set is reserved for final evaluation only. Using the test set for tuning leads to data leakage.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does 'precision' measure in a classification model?$q$, $j$["The percentage of actual positives correctly identified", "The percentage of predicted positives that are actually positive", "The overall accuracy of the model", "The number of false negatives"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Precision = TP / (TP + FP). It measures how many of the items the model labeled as positive are truly positive. High precision means few false alarms. Recall measures how many actual positives were caught.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which of the following best describes 'feature engineering'?$q$, $j$["The process of selecting the best ML algorithm", "The process of using domain knowledge to create, transform, or select input variables to improve model performance", "The process of collecting raw data", "Tuning model hyperparameters"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Feature engineering involves transforming raw data into meaningful input features that improve model performance. Examples include creating interaction terms, binning continuous variables, or extracting date components.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$In a confusion matrix for a binary classifier, what is a False Negative (FN)?$q$, $j$["A negative sample correctly predicted as negative", "A positive sample incorrectly predicted as negative", "A negative sample incorrectly predicted as positive", "A positive sample correctly predicted as positive"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$A False Negative occurs when the actual class is positive but the model predicts negative. In medical diagnosis, this would be telling a sick patient they are healthy — often the most dangerous type of error.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between a parametric and a non-parametric machine learning model?$q$, $j$["Parametric models are always better", "Parametric models have a fixed number of parameters regardless of training data size; non-parametric models grow with data", "Non-parametric models have no parameters", "Parametric models can only do regression"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Parametric models (like linear regression, logistic regression) have a fixed structure. Non-parametric models (like KNN, decision trees) grow in complexity with more data — they make fewer assumptions about the data distribution.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the role of a loss function in machine learning?$q$, $j$["It defines the model architecture", "It measures how far the model's predictions are from the true values, guiding the optimization process", "It splits data into train and test sets", "It determines the learning rate automatically"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$A loss function quantifies the error between predictions and ground truth. During training, optimization algorithms (like gradient descent) minimize the loss function to improve model accuracy.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the 'Elbow Method' used for in K-Means clustering?$q$, $j$["Detecting outliers in the data", "Determining the optimal number of clusters by plotting inertia vs. K and looking for a sharp bend", "Measuring cluster overlap", "Initializing cluster centroids"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The Elbow Method plots the within-cluster sum of squares (inertia) for different values of K. The 'elbow' point where the rate of decrease sharply slows suggests the optimal K value.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$Which statement best describes 'semi-supervised learning'?$q$, $j$["Learning with only labeled data", "Learning with a small amount of labeled data combined with a large amount of unlabeled data", "Learning by trial and error with rewards", "Learning without any data labels"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Semi-supervised learning sits between supervised and unsupervised learning. It leverages a small labeled dataset along with a much larger unlabeled dataset, which is cheaper to obtain.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is data normalization and why is it important?$q$, $j$["Removing duplicate records from data", "Scaling numerical features to a common range so that no single feature dominates due to its scale", "Converting categorical data to numbers", "Splitting data into train and test sets"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Normalization (or scaling) ensures all features contribute equally to the model. Without it, a feature with large values (e.g. income in thousands) would dominate over one with small values (e.g. age), skewing distance-based algorithms.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the fit() method do in a Scikit-learn estimator?$q$, $j$["Makes predictions on new data", "Trains the model by learning parameters from the training data", "Transforms the data without learning", "Evaluates model performance"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$fit() trains a model by computing the parameters (weights, centroids, etc.) from the training data. It modifies the estimator in-place and returns it. After fitting, predict() or transform() can be called.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between transform() and fit_transform() in Scikit-learn?$q$, $j$["They are identical", "transform() applies a pre-learned transformation; fit_transform() learns parameters AND applies them in one step", "fit_transform() is only for classifiers", "transform() is only used with PCA"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$transform() applies a transformation using already-learned parameters (must call fit() first). fit_transform() combines both steps — it learns the parameters from the data and transforms it simultaneously. Only use fit_transform() on training data.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is 'data leakage' in a machine learning pipeline?$q$, $j$["Data being deleted accidentally", "When information from the test set inadvertently influences the training process, leading to over-optimistic evaluation", "When training data is too small", "When a model reveals private user data"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Data leakage occurs when the test set influences model training or preprocessing (e.g. fitting a scaler on all data before splitting). This inflates performance metrics and leads to poor real-world results.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does MinMaxScaler do to the data?$q$, $j$["Standardizes to mean 0, std 1", "Scales each feature to a fixed range, typically [0, 1]", "Removes outliers from the data", "Rounds all values to the nearest integer"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$MinMaxScaler transforms each feature by scaling it to [0, 1] using the formula: (x - min) / (max - min). It preserves the original distribution shape but is sensitive to outliers.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$In Scikit-learn, what is the purpose of the make_pipeline() function?$q$, $j$["Creates a Pipeline from a list of steps without requiring named steps", "Connects two different machine learning models", "Generates synthetic training data", "Creates a ColumnTransformer automatically"]$j$::jsonb, 0, $q$make_pipeline() is a convenience function that creates a Pipeline without requiring you to name each step. The step names are auto-generated from the class names. It is equivalent to Pipeline() but more concise.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the n_components parameter control in PCA?$q$, $j$["The number of training iterations", "The number of principal components (dimensions) to keep after dimensionality reduction", "The number of clusters", "The regularization strength"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$n_components specifies how many principal components to retain. For example, PCA(n_components=2) projects high-dimensional data down to 2 dimensions while retaining as much variance as possible.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What problem does the IterativeImputer solve that SimpleImputer cannot?$q$, $j$["It handles categorical missing values", "It models each feature with missing values as a function of other features, capturing inter-feature relationships", "It is faster than SimpleImputer", "It handles missing values in the target variable"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$SimpleImputer fills missing values with a fixed statistic (mean, median) independently for each feature. IterativeImputer iteratively models each feature as a function of others, using their relationships to produce better estimates.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the silhouette score used for in clustering?$q$, $j$["Measuring the accuracy of a classifier", "Measuring how well each point fits its own cluster compared to neighboring clusters (no ground truth needed)", "Computing the optimal number of trees in Random Forest", "Evaluating regression model quality"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$The silhouette score ranges from -1 to 1. A high score means the point is well-matched to its own cluster and poorly matched to neighbors. It is an intrinsic metric requiring no ground truth labels.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between a classifier and a regressor in Scikit-learn?$q$, $j$["Classifiers are faster to train", "Classifiers predict discrete class labels; regressors predict continuous numerical values", "Regressors only work with scaled data", "Classifiers use fit(); regressors use train()"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Classifiers (e.g. LogisticRegression, DecisionTreeClassifier) predict categories. Regressors (e.g. LinearRegression, DecisionTreeRegressor) predict continuous values. Both use the same fit/predict API.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does cross_val_score() return?$q$, $j$["A single score for the entire dataset", "An array of scores, one for each fold in cross-validation", "The trained model after cross-validation", "The best hyperparameters found"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$cross_val_score() performs k-fold cross-validation and returns an array of k scores. You can then compute the mean and standard deviation to get a reliable performance estimate.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is regularization in machine learning?$q$, $j$["Normalizing input features", "Adding a penalty term to the loss function to discourage overly complex models and prevent overfitting", "Removing outliers from training data", "Splitting data into equal-sized batches"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Regularization penalizes model complexity during training. L1 (Lasso) adds the sum of absolute weights; L2 (Ridge) adds the sum of squared weights. Both reduce overfitting by discouraging large parameter values.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$In Scikit-learn, what does the random_state parameter ensure?$q$, $j$["The model will always perform better", "Reproducibility — the same result is produced each time the code runs", "The data is shuffled before training", "The model uses random features"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$random_state seeds the random number generator, making stochastic operations (train/test split, weight initialization, etc.) deterministic. Setting random_state=42 (or any fixed value) ensures reproducible results.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the difference between LabelEncoder and OrdinalEncoder in Scikit-learn?$q$, $j$["They are identical", "LabelEncoder is for encoding target labels (1D); OrdinalEncoder is for encoding input features (2D) with optional category ordering", "OrdinalEncoder only works with numbers", "LabelEncoder handles multiple columns at once"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$LabelEncoder encodes a 1D target column. OrdinalEncoder is designed for input feature matrices (2D) and lets you specify the category order, making it ideal for ordinal features like ['S', 'M', 'L', 'XL'].$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is stratified k-fold cross-validation?$q$, $j$["Cross-validation where folds are sorted by value", "Cross-validation that ensures each fold has the same class distribution as the full dataset", "Cross-validation with overlapping folds", "Cross-validation only for regression"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Stratified k-fold preserves the percentage of samples from each class in every fold. This is especially important for imbalanced datasets where random splitting might result in some folds having very few minority class samples.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is inertia in K-Means clustering?$q$, $j$["The number of iterations the algorithm takes to converge", "The sum of squared distances from each point to its assigned cluster centroid", "The distance between two cluster centroids", "The number of points in the largest cluster"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Inertia (within-cluster sum of squares) measures how spread out the points in each cluster are. Lower inertia means tighter clusters. It decreases as K increases and is used in the Elbow Method to choose K.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What advantage does a Scikit-learn Pipeline offer over applying transformations manually?$q$, $j$["Pipelines are always faster to train", "Pipelines prevent data leakage by ensuring preprocessing is only fitted on training data during cross-validation", "Pipelines automatically select the best model", "Pipelines reduce the number of features automatically"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$When used with cross-validation, a Pipeline ensures transformers are re-fit on each training fold only. Manual preprocessing before cross-validation would fit on all data including validation folds, causing data leakage.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the 'curse of dimensionality'?$q$, $j$["When a model has too many target variables", "The phenomenon where high-dimensional data becomes increasingly sparse, making distance-based algorithms less effective", "When the number of hyperparameters exceeds the number of samples", "When PCA removes too many dimensions"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$As the number of features increases, data becomes increasingly sparse in the feature space. Distance metrics become meaningless because all points appear equally far apart. Dimensionality reduction (PCA) helps address this.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$In Scikit-learn, what does the score() method of a trained classifier return by default?$q$, $j$["F1 score", "Mean accuracy on the given test data and labels", "Precision score", "AUC-ROC score"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$By default, score() on a classifier returns mean accuracy: the fraction of correctly classified samples. score() on a regressor returns R² by default. You can always use specific metric functions for other measures.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does DBSCAN stand for and what makes it different from K-Means?$q$, $j$["Data-Based Scanning; it requires specifying K clusters in advance", "Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise; it automatically finds clusters of arbitrary shape and identifies outliers without needing K", "Database Scan; it works only on tabular data", "Distance-Based Scanning; it uses Euclidean distance only"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$DBSCAN discovers clusters based on density. Unlike K-Means, it does not require specifying the number of clusters, can find non-spherical clusters, and labels low-density points as noise (outliers).$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of train_test_split() in Scikit-learn?$q$, $j$["To normalize the training data", "To randomly divide the dataset into separate training and testing subsets for unbiased model evaluation", "To remove missing values from the dataset", "To balance class distributions"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$train_test_split() splits arrays or matrices into train and test subsets. Evaluating on data the model has never seen provides an unbiased estimate of performance. The test_size parameter controls the split ratio.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the Dense layer in Keras do?$q$, $j$["Reduces the spatial dimensions of feature maps", "Applies a learned linear transformation where every input neuron is connected to every output neuron, followed by an activation function", "Performs convolution on image data", "Randomly drops neurons during training"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$A Dense (fully connected) layer connects every input to every output with learned weights: output = activation(dot(input, weights) + bias). It is the standard feedforward layer used in MLPs.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of the Dropout layer in a neural network?$q$, $j$["To speed up training by removing slow neurons", "To reduce overfitting by randomly setting a fraction of input units to zero during training", "To normalize the activations across a batch", "To reduce the number of output classes"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dropout randomly deactivates neurons during each training step, preventing the network from relying too heavily on any single neuron. This acts as a regularizer and reduces overfitting.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What activation function is typically used in the output layer of a multi-class classification network?$q$, $j$["ReLU", "Sigmoid", "Softmax", "Tanh"]$j$::jsonb, 2, $q$Softmax normalizes the output vector into a probability distribution over all classes that sums to 1. It is used for multi-class classification. Sigmoid is for binary classification outputs.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does model.compile() do in Keras?$q$, $j$["Trains the model on the data", "Configures the model for training by specifying the optimizer, loss function, and evaluation metrics", "Saves the model to disk", "Loads pre-trained weights"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$compile() sets up the training configuration before calling fit(). You specify the optimizer (e.g. Adam), loss function (e.g. categorical_crossentropy), and metrics (e.g. accuracy).$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the role of an epoch in model training?$q$, $j$["One forward pass through a single batch", "One complete pass through the entire training dataset", "The learning rate schedule", "The number of layers in the network"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$An epoch is one complete cycle through the full training dataset. Training for multiple epochs allows the model to see and learn from each example multiple times, improving weights iteratively.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the main advantage of the ReLU activation function over sigmoid?$q$, $j$["ReLU outputs probabilities between 0 and 1", "ReLU avoids the vanishing gradient problem and is computationally cheaper, enabling training of deeper networks", "ReLU is differentiable everywhere", "ReLU always produces sparse activations"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Sigmoid saturates at 0 and 1, causing very small gradients (vanishing gradients) in deep networks. ReLU = max(0, x) is linear for positive inputs, preserving gradient magnitude. This makes deeper networks trainable.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is batch size in neural network training?$q$, $j$["The total number of training examples", "The number of training examples processed in one forward/backward pass before updating model weights", "The number of epochs", "The size of each layer"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Batch size controls how many samples are processed before the weights are updated. Smaller batches are noisier but can generalize better. Larger batches are more stable but require more memory.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does the Conv2D layer in Keras do?$q$, $j$["Flattens a 2D image into a 1D vector", "Applies learnable convolutional filters to detect local spatial features (edges, textures) in image data", "Reduces image dimensions by taking the maximum in a window", "Normalizes pixel values to [0, 1]"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$Conv2D slides learnable filters across the input image, computing dot products to produce feature maps that detect patterns like edges and textures. It is the fundamental layer of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs).$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What is the purpose of the Flatten layer in a CNN?$q$, $j$["Reduces the number of filters", "Converts the multi-dimensional feature maps from convolutional layers into a 1D vector to feed into Dense layers", "Flattens the gradient during backpropagation", "Merges multiple input branches"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$After convolutional and pooling layers produce 3D feature maps (height × width × channels), a Flatten layer reshapes them into a 1D vector so they can be passed to Dense (fully connected) layers.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet'),
('AI Programming', 'mcq', $q$What does model.summary() show in Keras?$q$, $j$["The model's training history (loss and accuracy per epoch)", "A table showing each layer's name, output shape, and number of trainable parameters", "The model's predictions on the test set", "The current values of all weights"]$j$::jsonb, 1, $q$model.summary() prints a per-layer breakdown including layer type, output shape, and parameter count, plus total and trainable parameters. It is essential for verifying the architecture before training.$q$, 'unOfficial Sheet');